 **Nyquist (1928).** In telephony, Harry Nyquist asked how fast you can sample a signal before you lose it: a signal with no frequencies above $B$ hertz is completely determined by samples taken at or faster than **$2B$ per second** \cite[Nyquist (1928)]{nyquist_sampling}. (His 1924 paper, "Certain Factors Affecting Telegraph Speed", concerns bandwidth and signalling speed; the $2B$ criterion is from the 1928 paper, with earlier proofs by Whittaker (1915) and Ogura (1920).) The number of *distinct values* a signal can carry per unit time is therefore not a matter of opinion; it is a number, fixed by its bandwidth. "How much can pass?" had become a computable question.
 
