 ### Spontaneous Game Play
 
-LLMs trained on internet text, most of which is *not* game transcripts, can still play simple games surprisingly well. GPT-4 plays chess at roughly 1200–1500 ELO, plays Go at a weak amateur level, plays tic-tac-toe and Connect-4 to expert level, and can reason through poker, Mastermind, and Wordle. None of this was an explicit training target. The training data contains chess PGN files, Go game records, online game discussions, and (much more recently) generated game transcripts from earlier LLMs. What is striking is that the same model can play a game it has never been explicitly trained on, simply by being told the rules in natural language and asked to choose moves. The ability degrades rapidly with game complexity (a strong human chess player crushes GPT-4, and Go is essentially lost at any serious level), but the *existence* of the ability, at all, in a model trained on next-token prediction, was not predicted.
